    "title": "Vikings at Full Health on Latest Injury Report - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Ted Schwerzler ,\n5 hours ago Matt Krohn-Imagn Images via Reuters Connect We are just a couple of days away from the Minnesota Vikings kicking off against the Indianapolis Colts. The bye week was a chance to get healthy, and they did just that.\nAt 5-0, Kevin O’Connell’s team couldn’t have asked for a better start. They have a brutal stretch coming up , but Daniel Jones’ team shouldn’t factor into that.\nCarlie Irsay-Gordon made changes at the top of the organization this week. The Colts also come in at 2-4 after handing the Tennessee Titans their first win of the season, 26-20, last Sunday. That’s the opposite of the situation the Vikings are in.\nNo injuries of note for Minnesota Vikings Before the bye, the MN Vikings were without Christian Darrisaw and Jordan Addison among their key contributors . They got extra rest after the victory over the Saints , and it looks like it may have helped.\nAll eight players on Thursday’s injury report practiced in full, with Justin Jefferson, Brian O’Neill and James Pierre upgrading from limited participation Wednesday. Addison and Darrisaw have now both logged back-to-back full practices.\nRunning back Jordan Mason was lost after the first week of the season due to a fractured thumb. He is eligible to return from injured reserve and has had his practice window opened. With back-to-back full practices under his belt, it seems likely he rounds out the Vikings’ full complement of starting skill position players this week.\nWith only one game to his credit thus far, Mason has 15 carries for 59 yards and a touchdown. His return should give Aaron Jones some much-needed relief, and it’s still plausible the Vikings add running back help prior to the trade deadline too.\nColts walking wounded against MN Vikings The offense started to click for Kyler Murray and Kevin O’Connell against the New Orleans Saints, and it’s certainly not a bad thing that the Indianapolis Colts will be without Sauce Gardner on Sunday. Unlike Minnesota’s, the Colts’ injury report is loaded.\nIndianapolis has 17 players listed on its injury report, with eight either limited or not practicing Thursday. Coach Shane Steichen may be looking to piece things together for a matchup in which the Colts are 6.5-point underdogs.\nThe MN Vikings need to avoid falling into a trap game situation, but the Colts are definitely presenting them with a positive opportunity. Daniel Jones has had success at U.S. Bank Stadium before, but facing his former team there on Sunday won’t be easy.",
    "summary": "5 hours ago Matt Krohn-Imagn Images via Reuters Connect We are just a couple of days away from the Minnesota Vikings kicking off against the Indianapolis Colts. All eight players on Thursday’s injury report practiced in full, with Justin Jefferson, Brian O’Neill and James Pierre upgrading from limited participation Wednesday. His return should give Aaron Jones some much-needed relief, and it’s still plausible the Vikings add running back help prior to the trade deadline too. The MN Vikings need to avoid falling into a trap game situation, but the Colts are definitely presenting them with a positive opportunity.",
